if INTEL_MIPS

config INTEL_MIPS_VMB_SMP
	bool "Firmware mailbox CPU launch (VMB)"
	depends on SMP
	default y
	help
	  Bring up the secondary VPEs the way the boot loader on this SoC
	  expects. All four are already running when Linux is entered; the
	  resident loader parks the three it is not using and releases one on
	  request, through a mailbox at the bottom of RAM and a doorbell
	  interrupt. Neither the Coherence Manager nor the Cluster Power
	  Controller is touched, which is what makes this different from
	  MIPS_CPS and why MIPS_CPS cannot start these CPUs.

	  This needs MIPS_MT_SMP to be enabled as well, and says so at boot if
	  it is not. Without it the kernel cannot tell the two VPEs of a core
	  apart, and the interrupt controller misroutes everything aimed at
	  the odd one.

	  Say Y unless you are booting this SoC from something other than the
	  vendor boot chain.

choice
	prompt "Built-in device tree"
	help
	  Legacy bootloaders do not pass a DTB pointer to the kernel, so
	  if a "wrapper" is not being used, the kernel will need to include
	  a device tree that matches the target board.

	  The builtin DTB will only be used if the firmware does not supply
	  a valid DTB.

config DTB_INTEL_MIPS_GRX500
	bool "Intel MIPS GRX500 Board"
	select BUILTIN_DTB

config DTB_INTEL_MIPS_FRITZ7560
	bool "AVM FRITZ!Box 7560"
	select BUILTIN_DTB

config DTB_INTEL_MIPS_FRITZ7590
	bool "AVM FRITZ!Box 7590"
	select BUILTIN_DTB

endchoice

endif
